# iserv-proxy

This is a small shim for running GHC `iserv` interpreters across a network.
See the Cabal file for full documentation.

## Usage

See `local-example.sh` for a sense of how to use this.

## Pipe-mode lifecycle checks

The proxy owns the interpreter started by `--pipe`. It allows five seconds
for graceful Shutdown, reports unexpected child exits, and bounds cleanup
when the interpreter ignores TERM. On POSIX it closes inherited descriptors. Failed
protocol work, unexpected child exit, and cancellation terminate the
interpreter's process group, including descendants. Successful Shutdown
reaps the interpreter and closes its pipes.

Use `make help` for the build and test commands. Run the regression suite
with both RTS modes:

```shell
make verify BUILD_DIR=dist-threaded CABAL_FLAGS=--flags=threaded
make verify BUILD_DIR=dist-nonthreaded CABAL_FLAGS=--flags=-threaded
```

The tests need Python 3, POSIX signals, and GNU coreutils `timeout`.
They use a fake interpreter to check successful Shutdown, child failures,
cancellation, blocked writes, inherited descriptors, and surviving
processes. Each case has separate startup and
cleanup deadlines; `TIMEOUT` bounds the whole build or test command.

To test an existing binary, run `make test ISERV_PROXY=/path/to/iserv-proxy`.
Set `CHILD_PYTHON` to a native Python executable when the proxy runs through
an emulator. The TCP mode does not own a child and keeps its existing
signal behavior.
